WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.730
Most Node.js applications rely on npm packages,

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:06.115
and many of those packages rely on other packages.

00:00:06.115 --> 00:00:08.720
A direct dependency is one your application names.

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:11.700
A transitive dependency is pulled in by another dependency.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.700
The full dependency tree can become much larger than the code a team intentionally wrote,

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.338
which means supply chain security has to be part of normal application security.

00:00:19.338 --> 00:00:23.240
Lockfiles help make dependency versions more reproducible across installs and

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:25.565
environments. They are not a guarantee of safety,

00:00:25.565 --> 00:00:28.480
but they reduce ambiguity about what code should be installed.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.600
When lockfiles are ignored, regenerated casually, or allowed to drift without review,

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:34.900
teams can lose visibility into what changed.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:38.635
Dependency updates should be intentional enough to support both security fixes and

00:00:38.635 --> 00:00:39.795
operational stability.

00:00:39.795 --> 00:00:44.120
Supply chain risks include vulnerable packages, abandoned packages, typosquatting,

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:48.300
dependency confusion, and package scripts that execute during installation or build.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.600
These topics do not require fear, but they do require governance.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:55.500
Teams should know which package registries are trusted, how new dependencies are approved,

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.700
what scripts are allowed in CI/CD, and how vulnerability alerts are triaged.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.895
The answer is not to write everything from scratch.

00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:04.400
That usually creates more risk and maintenance burden.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.400
The practical answer is to minimize unnecessary dependencies,

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:11.000
prefer well-maintained packages, use software composition analysis,

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.000
review dependency changes, pin and update responsibly,

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.360
and design the application so a compromised or flawed package has less privilege and less

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:19.910
access than it would by default.
